{
 "id": "MW-09",
 "system": "ac3",
 "sessionId": "bm-ac2-mw-09-b12e151543",
 "endReason": "escalated",
 "customerTurns": 6,
 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Bosch built-in microwave BFL524MS0B randomly stops part way through cooking, time still on the display. Opening and closing the door lets it carry on. Maybe once every few days."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is the door interlock or control. Next: Check for a control/child lock and try a power-cycle reset. Let me know what you find."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I can't see a child lock indicator on the display, and no error codes. It only stops mid-programme, and it seems to happen more when I close the door gently. No smell, sparks or tripping either. Do you want me to try a power-cycle anyway?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, unplugging the microwave for a few minutes is a safe and standard first step that often resets the control board. It does not carry any risk and may resolve the issue if it was caused by a temporary electronic glitch. From what you have described, the most likely cause is supply / door-interlock / control [random loss]. Next: Note the timing (a few seconds vs several minutes vs random) and whether it gets hot. Let me know what you find."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "It's random, there's no set time into the cook, just every few days. Nothing else is affected and I haven't noticed it getting hot. It's a built-in so I can't easily unplug it, it's screwed into the tall unit. Is there another way to reset it, like at the fuse board?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "No, resetting the mains fuse or circuit breaker will not fix this issue as it is a safety interlock fault. The microwave stops because the door switch detects an open circuit when the door is not latched securely. With it unplugged, look at the plastic hooks on the edge of the door and the slots they click into, and check the door shuts cleanly — food or grease in the slots can stop them engaging. Was there food / grease in the way (and is it cleaned), is a hook or latch broken, or does it all look fine?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "There was some grease around the door hooks and latch slots, so I cleaned it. It still happened once since. No hooks look broken, but the door looks very slightly lower on the handle side, though I can't be sure. I can't unplug it or take it out of the housing."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is door interlock / monitor microswitch (electrical start path). When you press the door-release button, does it feel normal, loose/floppy, or unusually stiff?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I haven't noticed anything odd with the release button, it feels normal as far as I can tell. I can't compare it to how it felt when new though."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Plug it back in and try a cup of water for a minute. Does the door latch and the microwave start normally now, or still not?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I can't unplug it as it's screwed into the tall unit, and it's not stopped now anyway, it heats normally most of the time. The fault only shows up every few days. Is there anything else I can check, or should I get a Bosch engineer in? I'd pay for a visit."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Given that you cannot unplug the appliance or remove it from its housing, and the issue involves the door latch mechanism which is critical for safety, you should get a Bosch engineer in. Attempting further checks without proper access could be unsafe or ineffective. Next: The customer can visually inspect the accessible latch area. Let me know what you find."
  }
 ],
